# Applies to `npm start` only. Committed on purpose — every teammate needs it.
#
# Why: react-dev-utils derives webpack-dev-server's allowedHosts from the machine's
# IP, but keeps it only when that IP is in a private range (10.x, 172.16–31.x,
# 192.168.x). On any other network — carrier-grade NAT (100.64.x), a VPN, a campus
# or hotel network — it discards the value and CRA starts with `allowedHosts:
# [undefined]`, which fails schema validation:
#
#   Invalid options object. Dev Server has been initialized using an options object
#   that does not match the API schema.
#    - options.allowedHosts[0] should be a non-empty string.
#
# The strict branch is only taken because package.json declares a `proxy`.
DANGEROUSLY_DISABLE_HOST_CHECK=true

# Bind to loopback only. Without this the dev server listens on every interface, so
# on a public network anyone on it can reach your source and your proxied backend.
# It also removes the DNS-rebinding exposure the flag above would otherwise open.
# Need to open the app on your phone? Start once with: HOST=0.0.0.0 npm start
HOST=127.0.0.1
